    <div class="profile-section-block" id="foundation">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Foundation</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/tomas-garrigue-masaryk"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/tomas-garrigue-masaryk.jpg" alt="Tomáš Garrigue Masaryk" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/tomas-garrigue-masaryk">Tomáš Garrigue Masaryk</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tom%C3%A1%C5%A1_Garrigue_Masaryk_1925.PNG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unknown author, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Czechoslovakia was created as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austro-hungarian-empire">Austria-Hungary</a> collapsed at the end of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a>. Its case had been made abroad by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tomas-garrigue-masaryk">Tomáš Garrigue Masaryk</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edvard-benes">Edvard Beneš</a> and the Slovak astronomer and general <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/milan-rastislav-stefanik">Milan Rastislav Štefánik</a>, whose <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/czechoslovak-national-council">Czechoslovak National Council</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> won Allied recognition in 1918, helped by the fame of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/czechoslovak-legion">Czechoslovak Legion</a>, which had fought its way along the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trans-siberian-railway">Trans-Siberian Railway</a> during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russian-civil-war">Russian Civil War</a>. On 18 October 1918 Masaryk issued a declaration of independence in Washington, and on 28 October the National Committee in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prague">Prague</a> proclaimed the new state as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburg</a> authority dissolved. Two days later Slovak leaders meeting at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/martin-slovakia">Turčiansky Svätý Martin</a> declared their wish to join a common state. The Revolutionary National Assembly elected Masaryk president in November 1918. Frontiers were fixed only after fighting: troops occupied the German-speaking borderlands of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bohemia">Bohemia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moravia">Moravia</a>, defended <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slovakia">Slovakia</a> against the Hungarian Soviet Republic in 1919 and clashed with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> over <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cieszyn">Teschen</a>, which was divided in 1920. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-saint-germain-en-laye-1919">Treaty of Saint-Germain</a> added <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carpathian-ruthenia">Subcarpathian Ruthenia</a>, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-trianon">Treaty of Trianon</a> in 1920 set the border with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a>. The constitution of February 1920 completed the foundation of the First Republic.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="socioeconomy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Socioeconomy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/plzen"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/plzen.jpg" alt="Plzeň" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/plzen">Plzeň</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Plze%C5%88_Montage_I.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Isiwal Ra..., CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The new state was a land of several nations. The 1921 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a> counted about 13.6 million people, of whom <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/czechs">Czechs</a> and Slovaks, officially treated as one Czechoslovak nation, formed about two-thirds; there were also more than three million Germans, about 750,000 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magyars">Hungarians</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rusyns">Ruthenes</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poles">Poles</a>. The Czech lands had been the industrial heart of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburg</a> monarchy, and Czechoslovakia inherited a large share of its industry, from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/skoda">Škoda Works</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/plzen">Plzeň</a> to textile, glass, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sugar-plantation">sugar</a> and brewing concerns, while <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tomas-bata">Tomáš Baťa</a> built a world-scale shoe business at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zlin">Zlín</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slovakia">Slovakia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carpathian-ruthenia">Ruthenia</a> remained largely agrarian and poorer. A land reform broke up great estates, many of them owned by German and Hungarian nobles. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-depression">Great Depression</a> struck the export industries of the German borderlands especially hard, feeding resentment there. Under communist rule after 1948 the economy was nationalised, collectivised and redirected towards heavy industry and trade within <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/comecon">Comecon</a>; living standards rose but stagnated from the 1960s. Religious life was mainly Catholic, with strong Protestant and secular traditions in the Czech lands. Cultural life was rich: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karel-capek">Karel Čapek</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leos-janacek">Leoš Janáček</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jaroslav-hasek">Jaroslav Hašek</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/czechoslovak-new-wave">Czechoslovak New Wave</a> in film and later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/milan-kundera">Milan Kundera</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vaclav-havel">Václav Havel</a>.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="military">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Military</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/zb-vz-26"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/zb-vz-26.jpg" alt="ZB vz. 26" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/zb-vz-26">ZB vz. 26</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:ZB_vz._26_at_Great_Patriotic_War_museum_in_Smolensk.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Vitaly V. Kuzmin, CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Czechoslovak army grew out of the wartime legions and the troops that secured the frontiers in 1918–1920. The First Republic relied on alliances: the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/little-entente">Little Entente</a> with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yugoslavia">Yugoslavia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romania">Romania</a>, a treaty with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> in 1924 and a pact with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a> in 1935 that obliged <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a> to help only if France acted first. Its arms industry was among the most advanced in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zb-vz-26">ZB vz. 26</a> light machine gun from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brno">Brno</a> became the model for the British <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bren-light-machine-gun">Bren gun</a>. From 1935 the state built a chain of border fortifications facing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, and in September 1938 it mobilised well over a million men. At <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/munich">Munich</a> the great powers decided its fate without it, and the army surrendered the fortified <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sudetenland">Sudetenland</a> without a fight. During the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a> Czechoslovak airmen flew with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/royal-air-force">Royal Air Force</a>, a battalion fought at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tobruk">Tobruk</a>, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/1st-czechoslovak-army-corps">1st Czechoslovak Army Corps</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ludvik-svoboda">Ludvík Svoboda</a> fought alongside the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-army">Red Army</a>, including at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dukla-pass">Dukla Pass</a> in 1944. After 1948 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/czechoslovak-peoples-army">Czechoslovak People's Army</a> became a pillar of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/warsaw-pact">Warsaw Pact</a>, and Czechoslovak arms went to Israel in 1948 and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> in 1955. In August 1968 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/warsaw">Warsaw</a> Pact troops invaded unopposed, and Soviet forces stayed until 1991.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="governance">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Governance &amp; Law</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/edvard-benes"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/edvard-benes.jpg" alt="Edvard Beneš" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/edvard-benes">Edvard Beneš</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Edvard_Bene%C5%A1.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unknown . Transfer; United States. Office of War Information. Overseas Picture Division. Washington Division; 1944., Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The First Republic was a parliamentary democracy with a bicameral National Assembly, proportional representation and a strong presidency shaped by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tomas-garrigue-masaryk">Masaryk</a>, who served from 1918 to 1935 and was succeeded by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edvard-benes">Beneš</a>. Coalitions of the main Czech and Slovak parties, the so-called <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/petka">Pětka</a>, managed government, and German parties joined cabinets from 1926. It was the only democracy in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-europe">Central Europe</a> to last until 1938, but centralism angered Slovak autonomists such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/andrej-hlinka">Andrej Hlinka</a>, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sudeten-german-party">Sudeten German Party</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/konrad-henlein">Konrad Henlein</a> won more votes than any other party in 1935. After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/munich">Munich</a> a federalised Second Republic lasted until March 1939. A government-in-exile under Beneš in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> was recognised by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/allies">Allies</a>, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kosice-programme">Košice Programme</a> of 1945 set the post-war order. The Communist Party of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/klement-gottwald">Klement Gottwald</a>, the largest party in the 1946 elections, took full power in February 1948. Thereafter the party ruled through its Central Committee and the security police, staging show trials such as that of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rudolf-slansky">Rudolf Slánský</a> in 1952. The 1960 constitution renamed the state the Czechoslovak Socialist Republic, and from 1 January 1969 it became a federation of Czech and Slovak republics, the one reform of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prague-spring">Prague Spring</a> that survived.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="impact">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Global &amp; Regional Impact</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/lidice"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/lidice.jpg" alt="Lidice" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/lidice">Lidice</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Lidice_CZ_new_Lidice_municipal_office.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Miaow Miaow, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Czechoslovakia was the clearest product of the post-1918 principle of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/self-determination">national self-determination</a> and became a measure of its successes and failures. For two decades its democracy, cultivated by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tomas-garrigue-masaryk">Masaryk</a> as a moral as well as political project, set it apart from the authoritarian regimes around it, and the betrayal at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/munich">Munich</a> in 1938 became a lasting lesson of the failure of appeasement. The occupation brought the persecution and murder of most of the country's <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a>, while the assassination of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/reinhard-heydrich">Reinhard Heydrich</a> in 1942 and the destruction of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lidice">Lidice</a> in reprisal became symbols of resistance and terror. The post-war expulsion of about three million Germans under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benes-decrees">Beneš decrees</a> ended centuries of Czech-German coexistence in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bohemia">Bohemia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moravia">Moravia</a> and remains a sensitive subject. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prague-spring">Prague Spring</a> of 1968, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-dubcek">Alexander Dubček</a>'s attempt at 'socialism with a human face', and its suppression shaped reform communism and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eurocommunism">Eurocommunism</a> across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jan-palach">Jan Palach</a>'s self-immolation in January 1969 became an emblem of protest. Charter 77 and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vaclav-havel">Václav Havel</a>'s essays on 'living in truth' influenced dissidents throughout the Soviet bloc. Finally, the peaceful '<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dissolution-of-czechoslovakia">Velvet Divorce</a>' of 1992 offered a rare example of a state dissolving by negotiation rather than war, in sharp contrast to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yugoslavia">Yugoslavia</a>.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="decline">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Decline &amp; Fall</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/gustav-husak"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/gustav-husak.jpg" alt="Gustáv Husák" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/gustav-husak">Gustáv Husák</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Gust%C3%A1v_Hus%C3%A1k_-_o%C5%99%C3%ADznuto.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">kalendarakcii.bratislava.sk, CC BY-SA 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Communist rule in Czechoslovakia ended suddenly. After the crushing of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prague-spring">Prague Spring</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gustav-husak">Gustáv Husák</a> presided over two decades of 'normalisation', with purges, censorship and economic stagnation. When the police broke up a student march in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prague">Prague</a> on 17 November 1989, mass demonstrations and a general strike followed, organised by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/civic-forum">Civic Forum</a> in the Czech lands and Public Against Violence in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slovakia">Slovakia</a>. Within weeks the party gave up its leading role, and on 29 December 1989 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vaclav-havel">Václav Havel</a> was elected president, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-dubcek">Dubček</a> as chairman of the Federal Assembly. Free elections followed in June 1990. Yet the end of communism exposed old Czech-Slovak differences, visible in the 'hyphen war' over the state's name, settled in April 1990 as the Czech and Slovak Federative Republic. Slovak politicians wanted looser ties and a gentler economic transition than the rapid market reforms and voucher privatisation favoured in Prague. The June 1992 elections were won by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vaclav-klaus">Václav Klaus</a> in the Czech lands and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vladimir-meciar">Vladimír Mečiar</a> in Slovakia, who agreed to separate. Slovakia declared <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sovereignty">sovereignty</a> in July, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/havel">Havel</a> resigned, and the Federal Assembly voted in November to dissolve the federation. Czechoslovakia ceased to exist on 31 December 1992, succeeded on 1 January 1993 by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/czech-republic">Czech Republic</a> and Slovakia.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>Czechoslovakia was the most durable democracy of interwar <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-europe">Central Europe</a> and an industrial power whose abandonment at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/munich">Munich</a> in 1938 opened the way to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a>, then a Soviet-bloc state whose <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prague-spring">Prague Spring</a> and Velvet Revolution shaped European politics before it dissolved peacefully in 1992.</em></p>

